# Retracted figures -- numbers this project has WITHDRAWN, and what replaced
# them. Swept by scripts/retracted-figures.sh.
#
# FORMAT, one per line:
#     <retracted-literal> -- <replacement-literal> -- <where it was retracted>
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. `scripts/fact-health.sh` reads only *.md and matches
# only ledger IDs, so a figure restated inside a .py string is invisible to it
# twice over. Measured 2026-08-18: J-040 recounted the whisper metric and the
# correction reached the journal, the runbook and the dossier, while four LIVE
# surfaces kept quoting the withdrawn pair -- including gates.py's REFUSAL
# text, so the gate argued from a number the project had already withdrawn.
#
# THE RULE THE SWEEP ENFORCES: a retracted literal may still appear in code or
# policy, but only NEXT TO its replacement -- that is what a correction looks
# like ("the first pass read 2329/6.8, but ..."). A lone occurrence is a stale
# restatement, and that is the failure. Window is 8 lines either way, which is
# a comment block.
#
# NOT SWEPT: docs/ (where the retraction is legitimately discussed at length)
# and .truth/claims.jsonl (append-only history -- an old record quoting a
# figure is a fact about what was believed then, never a restatement to fix).
#
# CHOOSE SPECIFIC LITERALS. Matching is on word boundaries, so a short or
# common number will collide with versions, hashes and ids. If a literal is
# noisy, make it longer rather than loosening the sweep.

